WEBVTT

00:09.077 --> 00:12.537
You're the one who will be 
teaching me this book, yes?

00:25.897 --> 00:28.597
<b>Jaadugar</b>

01:47.227 --> 01:48.807
Incredible.

01:48.807 --> 01:54.137
Tolui really found a copy 
of <i>Elements</i>, just for me.

01:49.317 --> 01:53.777
<b>Otchigin</b>

01:55.787 --> 01:57.287
What's your name?

01:59.547 --> 02:02.007
Fatima is me.

02:02.007 --> 02:05.147
I help you in studies, Highness.

02:05.667 --> 02:07.107
I look forward to it.

02:07.107 --> 02:08.197
But...

02:09.207 --> 02:12.937
What does Highness want with book?

02:12.937 --> 02:15.247
It holds not much value.

02:15.247 --> 02:16.097
What?

02:16.097 --> 02:18.287
"A point is that which has no part."

02:18.507 --> 02:21.237
"A line is breadthless length."

02:21.637 --> 02:23.607
All it does is state obvious.

02:23.607 --> 02:25.547
Not terribly useful.

02:27.477 --> 02:28.457
Is that so?

02:29.447 --> 02:32.967
<i>I can't say for sure why she wanted 
the book to begin with,</i>

02:32.977 --> 02:38.727
<i>but that prince, Tolui, 
didn't seem to understand its value.</i>

02:39.287 --> 02:42.897
<i>She might part ways with the book 
if she loses interest.</i>

02:43.227 --> 02:44.877
Stating the obvious.

02:45.437 --> 02:47.357
Yes, that's true.

02:47.657 --> 02:49.627
For your people, at least.

02:49.627 --> 02:50.357
What?

02:50.357 --> 02:54.987
But for us, this book marks the beginning.

02:54.987 --> 02:59.327
Of our journey to acquire western 
knowledge not found on the steppe.

02:59.547 --> 03:00.497
<i>Huh?</i>

03:01.167 --> 03:04.337
Tolui, my husband, is the Otchigin.

03:05.087 --> 03:08.267
He is the keeper of the hearth, 
tasked with watching over its fire.

03:08.757 --> 03:12.927
Even after his older brothers set off 
and received their own pastures,

03:13.247 --> 03:15.887
the youngest son remained
in the household

03:15.887 --> 03:19.137
to protect the family fortune 
and eventually inherit it.

03:19.637 --> 03:23.347
As the Otchigin, 
Tolui will one day become emperor.

03:23.987 --> 03:25.057
But well...

03:25.527 --> 03:28.277
He's not exactly the brightest
 at times, is he?

03:28.787 --> 03:32.487
And it's not just Tolui. The same 
can be said for many of our people.

03:32.487 --> 03:35.277
They know only of life here on the steppe.

03:36.767 --> 03:39.367
Vast as it may be, 
the world is vaster still.

03:40.097 --> 03:41.577
I wish to learn more.

03:42.147 --> 03:46.217
About the many lands 
within reach of Mongol hooves.

03:46.967 --> 03:52.207
Your people have predicted the movement 
of the stars since antiquity, yes?

03:52.607 --> 03:56.177
How curious it is that you can
figure out the size of the Earth.

03:57.637 --> 04:00.937
This person traveled
to the ends of the Earth?

04:00.937 --> 04:04.057
Of course not. He used geometry.

04:04.057 --> 04:06.687
What makes "obvious things" obvious?

04:06.967 --> 04:11.747
It is by amassing examples one by one
that we prove universal principles.

04:13.017 --> 04:16.617
Gaining knowledge to pass down 
to future generations.

04:17.427 --> 04:21.867
That is my role as the one 
who will one day be empress.

04:27.367 --> 04:32.257
<i>Oh, why is it that she...</i>

04:33.737 --> 04:36.467
<i>Why does she infuriate me so?</i>

04:37.067 --> 04:41.667
<i>You don't know anything about how 
that book came into your possession?</i>

04:42.017 --> 04:45.107
<i>You can't imagine the misery
 we must endure</i>

04:45.107 --> 04:50.147
<i>when thanking those who robbed us of
everything for the charity you now offer?</i>

04:50.577 --> 04:55.067
<i>You mean to say you know nothing 
of how I'm feeling as I sit here?</i>

04:55.707 --> 04:58.527
What's wrong? Do you feel unwell?

05:00.197 --> 05:02.997
No. I admire your mind.

05:04.617 --> 05:09.307
A pleasure to serve Highness. 
I shall do utmost.

05:10.047 --> 05:13.007
I'm fortunate to have your help.
Thank you in advance.

05:13.347 --> 05:16.007
I know you must be tired. 
You may leave for now.

05:17.277 --> 05:18.967
I excuse myself.

05:25.827 --> 05:29.287
Your wisdom is something 
we need greatly.

05:29.607 --> 05:34.367
<i>I'm ashamed that I wavered back then,
 if only for a moment.</i>

05:36.357 --> 05:37.527
<i>I must...</i>

05:41.117 --> 05:44.947
<i>I alone must not forget this anger.</i>

05:45.507 --> 05:46.917
<i>I will never yield...</i>

05:47.537 --> 05:48.747
<i>to that woman!</i>

06:10.907 --> 06:12.527
Welcome back, Fatima.

06:12.527 --> 06:16.157
I can't thank you enough 
for venturing out in this sandstorm.

06:16.847 --> 06:18.277
Think nothing of it.

06:18.727 --> 06:23.217
Here in Mongolia, spring arrives 
once the sandstorms have passed.

06:23.217 --> 06:25.807
How I miss the Persian spring.

06:26.547 --> 06:29.827
My mother hasn't adjusted well
 to life here.

06:33.347 --> 06:34.327
Fatima.

06:35.307 --> 06:37.817
Princess Sorghaghtani asks you 
to come to her tent.

06:37.817 --> 06:40.107
She'd like you to start 
teaching her <i>Elements</i>.

06:41.857 --> 06:43.597
Certainly.

07:01.237 --> 07:03.617
Fatima! What happened to your face?

07:04.297 --> 07:07.127
There are too many animals in Mongolia.

07:07.507 --> 07:09.907
They're nothing but a nuisance.

07:09.907 --> 07:11.607
Perhaps.

07:12.037 --> 07:14.997
But they can be quite charming 
once you get to know them.

07:16.427 --> 07:17.957
Can they now?

07:41.077 --> 07:43.257
Here, just a moment.

07:43.257 --> 07:45.867
It's already our second winter here.

07:46.267 --> 07:48.977
Your health has improved 
as of late, Mother.

07:48.977 --> 07:52.067
I've finally gotten used to life
 here in Mongolia.

07:52.687 --> 07:55.537
This cold weather's the one thing
 I can't get used to.

08:04.627 --> 08:05.757
Tchou, tchou!

08:09.237 --> 08:15.207
<b>Summer, A.D. 1224</b>

08:11.847 --> 08:16.327
<i>Three years after Sitara 
became Sorghaghtani's tutor...</i>

08:18.177 --> 08:21.647
<i>The Mongol army defeated
 the shah of the Khwarazm,</i>

08:21.647 --> 08:23.637
<i>a major power in Central Asia,</i>

08:23.637 --> 08:26.967
<i>and returned in triumph 
to their home camp on the steppe.</i>

08:28.397 --> 08:31.907
<i>To welcome the Mongol army,
Sorghaghtani and her court</i>

08:31.907 --> 08:36.747
<b>Tus</b>

08:32.187 --> 08:36.747
<i>traveled southwest of the Altai Mountains 
to the basin of the Emil River,</i>

08:36.747 --> 08:40.247
<i>where they held a feast and a hunt 
to celebrate their victory.</i>

08:41.577 --> 08:43.297
Father!

08:43.977 --> 08:45.747
Hulegu! Kublai!

08:45.747 --> 08:47.867
I killed a deer.

08:47.867 --> 08:49.757
And I killed a hare.

08:50.047 --> 08:52.147
You two truly take after your father.

08:52.147 --> 08:53.857
Father!

08:53.857 --> 08:56.007
It was my first time on a hunt.

08:56.007 --> 08:57.627
Well? Aren't I amazing?

08:57.627 --> 08:59.107
Yes, that's amazing,

09:01.207 --> 09:03.417
Prince Hulegu. Prince Kublai.

09:04.277 --> 09:07.697
Please get ready, 
for the Great Khan will be here soon.

09:07.697 --> 09:10.067
Fatima! Look at that!

09:10.067 --> 09:13.287
My, what a splendid deer.

09:13.287 --> 09:17.417
And taking down a nimble hare 
is no small feat either, Prince Kublai.

09:19.037 --> 09:21.547
All my practice with the bow has paid off.

09:21.827 --> 09:23.517
Who's the girl?

09:23.517 --> 09:27.697
She's the tutor for reading <i>Elements</i>
you found for me.

09:27.697 --> 09:30.007
Oh, the scholar's daughter?

09:30.577 --> 09:32.677
How is she? Does she perform
 her duties well?

09:32.677 --> 09:36.727
She is quite diligent in her work.
Not to mention, clever and thoughtful.

09:37.347 --> 09:41.637
I can only tell how young she is 
when she smiles so fetchingly.

09:42.367 --> 09:45.897
You've brought me not just <i>Elements</i>, 
but also a capable tutor.

09:45.897 --> 09:47.067
Why, of course.

09:47.327 --> 09:48.967
Don't I look dashing?

09:49.997 --> 09:51.157
A force to be reckoned with.

09:51.157 --> 09:57.087
It's tradition after a child's first hunt 
to rub the animal's fat on his fingers.

09:57.087 --> 09:59.577
The Great Khan himself
will do the honors.

09:59.577 --> 10:01.607
The Great Khan has arrived.

10:01.607 --> 10:03.217
Brother Kublai, let's go.

10:03.217 --> 10:04.047
Yeah!

10:14.517 --> 10:17.347
<i>The emperor of the Mongols, Genghis Khan.</i>

10:18.807 --> 10:22.267
<i>It was he who built this vast empire.</i>

10:22.607 --> 10:27.187
<i>In three years' time, the fates written
in the stars of those present here...</i>

10:27.917 --> 10:33.697
<i>would be set into grand motion by the fall
 of that glorious star, Genghis Khan.</i>

10:52.407 --> 10:56.467
<b>Summer, A.D. 1227</b>

11:00.887 --> 11:03.327
Rest assured, Great Khan.

11:04.197 --> 11:07.917
The future of the Mongols 
shall be as you willed it.

11:10.357 --> 11:11.447
Dad.

11:35.297 --> 11:39.407
<i>The fourth prince, Tolui, 
as the youngest brother and Otchigin,</i>

11:39.407 --> 11:42.057
<i>in accordance with 
the Mongol custom of ultimogeniture,</i>

11:42.057 --> 11:46.787
<i>inherited the majority of his father
 Genghis Khan's vast legacy.</i>

11:47.297 --> 11:48.937
<i>In addition to land and fortune,</i>

11:49.537 --> 11:55.837
<i>Tolui's family came to control 
almost all of the Mongol military.</i>

12:05.937 --> 12:07.087
It's Prince Tolui!

12:07.087 --> 12:07.837
Prince Tolui!

12:07.837 --> 12:08.827
Prince Tolui!

12:11.227 --> 12:17.427
<i>Following Genghis Khan's death, 
the empire needed to choose a new ruler.</i>

12:18.297 --> 12:21.917
<i>However, the tribal council known as
a kurultai, where the imperial clan</i>

12:21.917 --> 12:24.597
<i>and contending princes gather
 to elect a new emperor</i>

12:24.957 --> 12:28.587
<i>would not be called to convene 
for another two years.</i>

12:29.487 --> 12:32.427
<i>During this interim period, 
Tolui led not just the military,</i>

12:32.427 --> 12:35.417
<i>but all manner of state affairs 
while acting as regent.</i>

12:36.967 --> 12:43.867
<b>Autumn, A.D. 1229</b>

12:41.977 --> 12:44.607
It doesn't look to be 
in the best of health.

12:44.967 --> 12:47.687
Still, should be safe to eat.

12:47.687 --> 12:48.837
Let's slaughter it.

13:03.557 --> 13:05.407
That should do it.

13:06.027 --> 13:08.437
Keep on butchering away now.

13:08.437 --> 13:11.267
A massive feast like this 
is once in a lifetime.

13:12.817 --> 13:13.897
Here you are.

13:14.247 --> 13:16.137
Clean up these entrails for me.

13:18.057 --> 13:21.197
In the name of Allah, 
and Allah is the greatest.

13:22.657 --> 13:23.617
Fatima.

13:25.967 --> 13:29.247
Are you uncomfortable with butchering 
by cutting into the abdomen?

13:29.247 --> 13:31.627
Oh, I'm sorry. It's a habit of mine.

13:31.987 --> 13:36.547
I hear Muslims only eat animals 
that have had their throats cut.

13:36.547 --> 13:37.537
That's right.

13:37.537 --> 13:40.967
You spill the blood all over 
the ground that way, though.

13:40.967 --> 13:43.937
The worst part is draining the blood 
makes the meat lose its flavor.

13:43.937 --> 13:46.597
What a waste. The blood 
is what gives it flavor.

13:47.187 --> 13:50.807
Anyway, we do things differently here, 
so just try and put up with it.

13:51.107 --> 13:54.027
Don't worry. I'm used to it already.

13:54.517 --> 13:57.117
I've lived in Mongolia 
for eight years now.

13:57.117 --> 13:59.677
I've even come to enjoy 
eating unbled meat.

13:59.677 --> 14:00.487
Really?

14:01.317 --> 14:04.317
People who prefer the taste 
of meat that's been bled

14:04.317 --> 14:07.997
are like those who prefer sweets 
that aren't <i>too</i> sweet.

14:07.997 --> 14:09.437
Exactly!

14:09.437 --> 14:11.667
Okay, I'll leave you to those entrails.

14:11.667 --> 14:13.477
Thanks, Fatima.

14:13.477 --> 14:14.997
It's my pleasure.

14:19.467 --> 14:24.137
<i>There's a good reason behind butchering 
an animal by cutting its throat.</i>

14:24.777 --> 14:28.347
In the name of Allah, 
and Allah is the greatest.

14:31.287 --> 14:34.857
It's the most painless method
 for the animal.

14:35.197 --> 14:40.747
Meat that's been drained has a nicer color
and doesn't spoil as easily.

14:44.857 --> 14:49.407
<i>Still, it's no wonder I've gotten used 
to dark meat that hasn't been bled.</i>

14:50.627 --> 14:51.977
<i>Eight years.</i>

14:51.977 --> 14:58.297
<i>I've now been here in Mongolia
for as many years as I was in Tus.</i>

14:59.397 --> 15:03.297
<i>And I've yet to accomplish 
anything in my time here.</i>

15:04.107 --> 15:05.257
<i>That book...</i>

15:06.127 --> 15:11.137
<i>It's been within my grasp all these years,
 yet I haven't been able to take it back.</i>

15:12.117 --> 15:15.197
<i>One could hardly say 
I've reclaimed the book.</i>

15:20.067 --> 15:22.267
<i>There's something hard in here.</i>

15:27.967 --> 15:29.307
Could this be...

15:30.267 --> 15:32.967
Here, take this one as well.

15:32.967 --> 15:33.657
Sure.

15:35.627 --> 15:38.707
I still can't believe it's him.

15:38.707 --> 15:39.717
Right?

15:39.717 --> 15:42.837
Enough. Today's a day for celebration.

15:44.617 --> 15:47.557
It's the day our new emperor
 takes the throne.

16:18.857 --> 16:22.467
I hear the new emperor will call himself 
the "Kha'an" instead of "Khan."

16:22.807 --> 16:27.057
Bringing back an ancient title 
even Genghis Khan didn't adopt?

16:27.917 --> 16:29.887
The Mongols have risen to greatness.

16:29.887 --> 16:33.887
We're about to enter an age where Mongolia
 is the center of the world.

16:52.917 --> 16:56.127
The kurultai has entered 
its 41st day now.

16:56.127 --> 16:58.517
They must really be at loggerheads.

16:58.517 --> 17:01.667
What holds meaning here
 is the number 41.

17:02.227 --> 17:06.087
The kam's prophecy said the 41st day
 would be auspicious.

17:06.697 --> 17:11.927
That is, the prince will be nominated 
by the clan and take the throne...

17:12.967 --> 17:14.077
today.

17:16.827 --> 17:18.607
Onward, Great Kha'an.

17:18.667 --> 17:23.627
<b>Genghis Khan's
Younger Brother
Temüge</b>

17:18.667 --> 17:23.627
<b>Second Prince
Chagatai</b>

17:19.387 --> 17:20.317
To the throne.

17:20.657 --> 17:23.817
Take our hands, so we may lead you.

17:50.367 --> 17:52.227
Long live the new emperor!

17:52.687 --> 17:54.557
Long live the new emperor!

17:55.557 --> 17:58.337
Long live Ögedei Kha'an!

17:59.317 --> 18:01.397
Huzzah!

18:01.397 --> 18:03.727
Huzzah!

18:04.217 --> 18:09.677
Huzzah! Huzzah!

18:11.237 --> 18:12.277
Great Kha'an!

18:12.707 --> 18:15.937
Allow me to heartily congratulate you
 on taking the throne.

18:16.697 --> 18:18.997
Bravo, Brother Ögedei.

18:19.567 --> 18:21.207
Thanks, Tolui.

18:43.227 --> 18:45.777
Someday, there may be born
 among my progeny

18:46.087 --> 18:48.657
those who if rolled up in grass
would not be eaten by a cow,

18:49.227 --> 18:52.277
and if rolled up in fat
would not be eaten by a dog.

18:56.877 --> 19:01.677
Faced with an elk emerging crosswise 
and a mouse emerging lengthwise,

19:02.257 --> 19:03.777
they are sure to let both flee.

19:07.767 --> 19:10.737
Now <i>this</i> is cause for celebration.

19:10.737 --> 19:15.237
Dad's will is to be carried out, 
and I am free of governing duties.

19:17.647 --> 19:19.897
You did well filling in
 for all this time.

19:20.527 --> 19:21.807
Sorghaghtani.

19:22.497 --> 19:26.267
My only regret is I could not
 make you empress.

19:26.527 --> 19:28.047
Don't say that.

19:28.047 --> 19:32.237
I mean, when Dad shared his will with us, 
the thought crossed my mind.

19:32.857 --> 19:35.367
"Why? Shouldn't it have been me?"

19:35.887 --> 19:37.697
But this is how he replied.

19:38.497 --> 19:43.387
"When a many-headed snake tries 
to enter a burrow to outlast the cold,

19:43.387 --> 19:47.127
the heads fight each other, 
and it dies without making it inside."

19:47.417 --> 19:52.377
"Conversely, a serpent with one head
 will burrow inside and survive."

19:52.657 --> 19:56.347
"Ögedei, use your brains 
to become the head for us all."

19:59.247 --> 20:03.897
As the Otchigin, I thought 
he favored me the most.

20:04.447 --> 20:07.567
But that's not what Dad was thinking.

20:08.257 --> 20:12.317
The power he had given me 
was for more than just me.

20:13.077 --> 20:17.327
It is our bond as brothers 
that will truly make the Mongols strong.

20:18.117 --> 20:22.707
That is what my dad, 
Genghis Khan, wished for,

20:23.157 --> 20:25.247
and it's my greatest wish as well.

20:28.817 --> 20:30.747
<i>Your brotherly bond...</i>

20:41.257 --> 20:45.807
<i>It doesn't matter to me 
which of them becomes emperor,</i>

20:46.487 --> 20:49.647
<i>but breaking tradition 
just to have him succeed you?</i>

20:49.647 --> 20:53.277
<i>Is the prince named Ögedei 
so much more qualified?</i>

20:56.537 --> 20:58.487
<i>Seems Shira backed the wrong horse.</i>

21:01.277 --> 21:05.097
<i>In any case, the kurultai 
can now finally come to an end.</i>

21:12.157 --> 21:14.817
<i>Is this stone really what I think it is?</i>

21:15.137 --> 21:17.407
<i>I read about it in one 
of the mistress's books.</i>

21:17.407 --> 21:19.347
<i>It's extremely valuable.</i>

21:19.347 --> 21:20.307
Fatima.

21:22.727 --> 21:24.497
Princess Sorghaghtani?

21:27.657 --> 21:29.457
Wh-What's the matter?

21:31.307 --> 21:33.577
I have something important 
to discuss with you.

21:33.577 --> 21:34.607
With me?

21:34.887 --> 21:39.067
After today, I want you 
to be gone from my side.

21:40.067 --> 21:40.647
<i>What?</i>

21:41.197 --> 21:42.967
What? You're dismissing me?

21:42.967 --> 21:44.787
Have I done something wrong?

21:44.787 --> 21:46.727
That's not why.

21:46.727 --> 21:48.347
Th-Then, what could it be?

21:55.027 --> 21:58.007
Fatima. I want you...

22:00.237 --> 22:02.677
to act as my spy.

22:14.777 --> 22:18.617
Translation: John K.
Timing: Andy Ellis
Editing: SMR

22:14.777 --> 22:18.617
Quality Control: Alicia Ashby
Translation Check: Sriram Gurunathan

23:38.067 --> 23:43.257
<b>A Snake Shows Its Colors,
Man Hides His Colors</b>

23:38.077 --> 23:43.027
Next time: "A Snake Shows Its Colors, 
Man Hides His Colors."
